        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Chrome 110 applies User-Agent reduction to Navigator properties + `User-Agent` header]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/28275</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/28275"/>
        <updated>2025-11-07T14:33:52.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/47647?u=2fbfc431a85d357f00c0146d5e55b425b7f00a3d&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/>
<h4 dir="auto">Summary</h4>
<p dir="auto">Chromium browsers support <a href="https://privacysandbox.google.com/protections/user-agent" rel="nofollow">User-Agent reduction</a>, which means that features such as the <code class="notranslate">User-Agent</code> HTTP header provide less-specific information in an effort to reduce their effectiveness for fingerprinting/tracking.</p>
<p dir="auto">This PR adds data points covering User-Agent reduction to relevant web platform features that I know to be affected by the change.</p>
<p dir="auto">Compat data sources:</p>
<ul dir="auto">
<li>From looking at <a href="https://blog.chromium.org/2021/09/user-agent-reduction-origin-trial-and-dates.html" rel="nofollow">https://blog.chromium.org/2021/09/user-agent-reduction-origin-trial-and-dates.html</a>, it looks like rollout of UA reduction to 100% of Chromium desktop users occurred in version 110, while the version was 113 for Chromium Android users. I asked <a class="user-mention notranslate" data-hovercard-type="user" data-hovercard-url="/users/miketaylr/hovercard" data-octo-click="hovercard-link-click" data-octo-dimensions="link_type:self" href="https://github.com/miketaylr">@miketaylr</a> about this, and he confirmed it to be correct.</li>
<li>From looking at <a href="https://www.chromium.org/updates/ua-reduction/#applicable-platforms" rel="nofollow">https://www.chromium.org/updates/ua-reduction/#applicable-platforms</a>, it looks like Android WebView should be <code class="notranslate">false</code></li>
</ul>
<p dir="auto">Another question for Mike: For all of these data points, I used <a href="https://wicg.github.io/ua-client-hints/#user-agent" rel="nofollow">https://wicg.github.io/ua-client-hints/#user-agent</a> as the <code class="notranslate">spec_url</code>. Are there better spec URLs we can point to for these individual features?</p>
<p dir="auto">This PR is part of the final set of privacy sandbox information to be added to MDN. Much of it is being removed, but this feature is stable and will be kept, so it should be added.</p>

<h4 dir="auto">Test results and supporting details</h4>


<h4 dir="auto">Related issues</h4>

]]></content>
